WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:06.600
Did you know you can run Linux on a mainframe? Yes&nbsp;
- Linux! You might think that mainframes are only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:12.760
for big banks, airlines, insurance companies. And&nbsp;
that may have been true years ago. But nowadays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.960
everyone, including small and medium businesses,&nbsp;
are into big data. And to play in the big data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:25.200
playground, they need the infrastructure, tools&nbsp;
and software to do that. So I want to explain how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.440
Linux on the mainframe can help you deal with that&nbsp;
big data from a hardware viewpoint. But first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:37.600
I need to dispel a couple of myths. So the first&nbsp;
myth: Is there some sort of special kind of Linux&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.040
that you need to run on the mainframe? The truth&nbsp;
is you can run any version of Linux that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:49.440
familiar with. You can run your Red Hat Enterprise&nbsp;
Linux, the SUSE Linux Enterprise server, Ubuntu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.240
Debian, Fedora. Yes, even all these little&nbsp;
community distributions are possible to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:00.240
there. And additionally, even though the mainframe&nbsp;
has very special components related to storage and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.760
networking and lots and lots of different options,&nbsp;
inside of Linux they all look like you'd expect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.880
There's no proprietary tooling in order to install&nbsp;
on Linux to be able to see the block device. Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.320
network devices are just going to show up like&nbsp;
regular network devices. The second myth is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.640
mainframes are huge. So you may have seen in&nbsp;
movies, you know, they bring in the giant wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.520
of lights. Or if you're going back a little&nbsp;
further, the mainframe rooms that are huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.760
they have mainframe servers that look like&nbsp;
furniture, basically, or those spinning disks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:40.000
It's not like that anymore. A standard mainframe&nbsp;
these days fits into a 19 inch rack spot. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.360
some of the latest ones are even rack mountable&nbsp;
and so you can just put them in the existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:51.320
racks that you have. But that doesn't mean they're&nbsp;
not special. I have gone to great lengths to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:57.280
that they are. So what does make these mainframes&nbsp;
special? So the first thing that I want to point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:04.320
out is that in order to make this all happen, we&nbsp;
need a special chipset. This is a custom made for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.520
the IBM Z mainframes. And I just mentioned another&nbsp;
term, "IBM Z". So when you're looking into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:16.280
infrastructure and looking into mainframe, you may&nbsp;
see a few different terms. You may see s390x, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:21.080
may see IBM Z, you may see Telum when referring&nbsp;
to the processor. And these are all kind of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:27.360
same idea, it's a specific hardware architecture&nbsp;
built around this chip. So if we were to draw a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:35.600
little chip here with its processor cores, it's&nbsp;
everything that that's built on top of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.440
and Linux is is compiled for this, and it's got&nbsp;
a few special things to it. So the first one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:48.800
that it's got this thing we call the CPACF,&nbsp;
which is the "CP Assist for Cryptographic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.160
Functions" and that allows you to do a lot of&nbsp;
the cryptographic functions. Like in Linux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.680
you would think of something like OpenSSL. OpenSSL&nbsp;
is used by everything, and that can be leveraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:05.680
by the CPU core built right in there. The next&nbsp;
one I wanted to mention was the NXU, and that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.000
the "Nest Accelerator Unit" and that allows you&nbsp;
to do the compression and decompression, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.600
on the hardware itself. In Linux, you would&nbsp;
think about that as something like when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:20.320
using gzip in your applications or when you're&nbsp;
compressing and decompressing data. And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:27.480
that does is it takes away from the amount of work&nbsp;
that your general processors are doing. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:34.000
you've got your AI accelerator. So the AI&nbsp;
accelerator takes your AI workloads and processes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:40.360
them on a specific chip and allows them access&nbsp;
to the cache of the rest of your processors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:46.200
And that means that the work is prioritized to&nbsp;
some degree, but it also is not taking away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:52.880
necessarily general computing power. I also wanted&nbsp;
to mention something that is not on the CPU, but is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:59.040
part of the rest of the system. So on a mainframe,&nbsp;
you've got these big drawers full of cards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.400
do various things. They connect the mainframe to&nbsp;
the rest of the infrastructure in the data center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.760
including storage and other networking devices.&nbsp;
But one of the really important ones for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.600
is that it includes this thing called a Crypto&nbsp;
Express card. And the Crypto Express card is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.400
hardware security module. The idea of a hardware&nbsp;
security module (HSM) is you want to keep your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:23.400
cryptographic keys really, really safe. And kind&nbsp;
of the the example that a lot of organizations say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.680
is like, "Oh, I've got this outsider threat, this&nbsp;
bad guy who wants to get access to my data". Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.080
that's not the only threat you've got. It&nbsp;
turns out a lot of organizations are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:38.400
worried about the internal threat. So this could&nbsp;
be people working on the infrastructure who need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.120
to know about the keys and they need to know&nbsp;
about the data, but they don't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:49.920
need direct access to those things. So they&nbsp;
can use and access a key inside of your HSM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.880
but they don't actually technically have&nbsp;
access to using that key to decrypt data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.920
and then having access to that customer data. So&nbsp;
obviously I am a big fan of mainframe hardware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.040
but maybe you're not quite ready to go out and buy&nbsp;
one today. So instead, what I'm going to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.680
to you is you join us over on the IBM Linux One&nbsp;
Community Cloud. That allows you to get access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:16.360
a VM running Linux. Various distributions are&nbsp;
available to you for 120 days to play around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.520
maybe with your application to load up, you know,&nbsp;
a little website or something that you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:25.640
play around with. I like to cat the processor&nbsp;
CPU info and then show it to my friends like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.760
"Oh, I got to be on a mainframe". Whatever you'd&nbsp;
like to do. I hope you'll join me and check it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:51.600
out. Thanks for watching. Before you leave,&nbsp;
please remember to hit like and subscribe.

